Take down api/: the Azure sign-in was a second identity system we are not going to have

> So right now we've got this API folder, which to me looks like an Azure
> function. I'm gonna say let's take it down. I don't know what it was doing. It
> looked like a login feature, but it does not work the way we're gonna work. So
> I want it gone.

It was a login feature. `api/src/functions/GetRoles.js` is the whole membership
mechanism of the **Azure** path: Static Web Apps signs somebody in through Entra
ID, calls `GetRoles` once with their email, bakes whatever roles come back into
the session, and `staticwebapp.config.json` gates routes on them.

It was a stub. The lookup was a `TODO` with three options listed and none
chosen, so it has never returned a role to anybody.

And it is the wrong shape regardless, which is the actual reason to remove it
rather than finish it: **FCPM's identity is passkeys through the broker in
`worker/`** — enrolment, assertion, signed claims, all built and tested. Keeping
a second sign-in belonging to a host we do not deploy to means two answers to
"who is this person", and the one that was never wired up is the one sitting in
the config.

## What went with it, and why it had to

Deleting the function on its own would leave configuration describing a login
that cannot happen, which reads as a working feature to the next person. So:

- **`.github/workflows/deploy.yml`** loses `api_location: "api"`. It named the
  folder.
- **`site/staticwebapp.config.json`** loses `auth.rolesSource`, the `/login` and
  `/logout` rewrites, the two `.auth` 404s, the `/members/*` and `/api/members/*`
  role gates, and the `401 → /login` override.

**None of that was reachable.** There is no `/members/` area in this repository —
`membership.md` is a public page — and nothing anywhere links `/login` or
`/logout`. The config was gating routes that do not exist behind a sign-in
nothing offered.

What is left in that file is redirects, headers, the 404 fallback and the
trailing-slash rule, which is all it was ever doing for the site that actually
publishes.

## Verified

- Builds. The generated `staticwebapp.config.json` is **valid JSON** — worth
  saying because removing the role-gate lines moved where the Liquid loop's
  comma has to go, so the separator is now emitted by `forloop.last` instead of
  riding on a fixed entry above it.
- **49 redirects in, 49 out, on both hosts.** `site/_data/redirects.yml` has 49
  entries, `_redirects` has 49 lines and the SWA config has 49 routes. The two
  hosts still cannot drift.
- `test_no_secrets`, `test_tokens` and the broker's 119 tests pass.
- `NODE.md`'s three mentions of `api/` are updated in the same commit, since
  this is what made them wrong.

## Not done here, and worth a separate decision

**The rest of the Azure path is still present** — `deploy.yml` still builds and
would still deploy to Static Web Apps if `AZURE_STATIC_WEB_APPS_API_TOKEN` were
ever set, and `staticwebapp.config.json` is still generated. I left it because
you asked for `api/` specifically, and because `deploy.yml` is the build gate on
every pull request.

If Cloudflare Workers is the answer and Azure is not coming back, that whole
path can go too and the build gate can move into `smoke.yml`, which already
builds. Say the word and it is a small PR.
